<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>Getting the ground level for a paver walkway is one of those steps that looks simple until you’re actually standing in the yard with a level in one hand and a rake in the other, realizing the ground has more dips and high spots than it appeared to from the porch. Level ground is what makes the difference between pavers that sit flush and even versus a walkway that rocks underfoot or develops a wavy surface within the first season.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we level ground for paver walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, on nearly every project we build, and it’s a step that rewards patience far more than speed. This guide walks through what leveling actually means as distinct from excavation or grading, how to deal with an uneven starting point, and the specific tools and techniques for getting it right.</p>
<h2><b>Leveling Versus Excavation and Grading: What’s the Difference</b></h2>
<p>These three terms get used almost interchangeably by homeowners, but they refer to different stages of the same overall process.</p>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Term</b></td><td><b>What It Means</b></td><td><b>When It Happens</b></td></tr><tr><td>Excavation</td><td>Removing soil to the correct overall depth</td><td>First step, before any base material goes in</td></tr><tr><td>Grading</td><td>Establishing the intended slope or flatness of the site</td><td>During and after excavation, sets the overall plan</td></tr><tr><td>Leveling</td><td>Fine-tuning the surface to be flat and even at each stage</td><td>Happens at multiple points, base, sand, and paver placement</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Excavation is the broad work of digging out the area to the right depth. Grading is about establishing the intended slope, whether that’s perfectly flat or a deliberate gentle grade for drainage. Leveling is the finer, more precise work that happens on top of that grading, making sure the actual surface at each layer, the compacted base, the bedding sand, and finally the pavers themselves, is even and consistent rather than just roughly in the right shape. You can grade a site correctly and still end up with an unlevel walkway if the leveling work at each layer isn’t done carefully.</p>
<h2><b>Dealing With an Uneven or Bumpy Yard Before You Start</b></h2>
<h3><b>Walk the Site First</b></h3>
<p>Before any digging, walk the planned walkway path slowly and note any obvious high spots, dips, rocks, or root systems by eye. This gives you a general sense of what you’re working with before you’re committed to a specific excavation depth.</p>
<h3><b>Remove Obstacles Before Establishing Your Base Grade</b></h3>
<p>Rocks, exposed roots, and old debris need to come out before you can establish a reliable base grade underneath. Trying to level around an obstacle rather than removing it usually creates a weak point in the base that shows up as a dip or soft spot later, even if the surface looks fine on installation day.</p>
<h3><b>Address Low Spots and High Spots Separately</b></h3>
<p>A yard with both dips and raised areas along the walkway path needs different treatment for each. High spots typically need to be cut down during excavation, while low spots may need additional fill material, ideally compacted in layers rather than dumped in all at once, to avoid creating a soft, poorly compacted pocket that settles unevenly later.</p>
<h3><b>Check for Drainage Patterns Before Leveling</b></h3>
<p>An uneven yard often has a reason behind the unevenness, sometimes it’s natural settling, sometimes it’s related to how water has moved across that area over time. Before leveling everything flat, it’s worth understanding whether any part of the unevenness is doing useful drainage work that you’d want to preserve or redirect deliberately, rather than simply flattening without considering where water will go afterward.</p>

